WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.275
Here is a helper that every service has.

2
00:00:05.275 --> 00:00:08.559
It runs a block of code, and logs how long it took.

3
00:00:08.767 --> 00:00:09.777
Here is a call.

4
00:00:09.777 --> 00:00:14.625
The lambda uses amountMinor from outside its braces, so it is a closure.

5
00:00:14.833 --> 00:00:16.489
Count what one call costs.

6
00:00:16.489 --> 00:00:21.012
Here is the call site, read back from the bytecode and written as Java.

7
00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:25.465
First, invokedynamic builds a small object, a Function0.

8
00:00:25.465 --> 00:00:27.827
It holds a copy of amountMinor.

9
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:30.505
Then the call goes to timedNotInline.

10
00:00:30.505 --> 00:00:36.056
Inside it, block() calls that object's invoke method, a call through an interface.

11
00:00:36.267 --> 00:00:41.428
The helper is generic, so the result comes back as an Object, a boxed Long.

12
00:00:41.428 --> 00:00:43.561
It has to be unboxed to a long.

13
00:00:43.561 --> 00:00:48.447
That is one object, one interface call and one unboxing, on every call.

14
00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:51.080
Now mark the helper inline.

15
00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:54.113
The body stays the same, and so does the call.

16
00:00:54.333 --> 00:01:01.871
At every call site, the compiler copies the function's body, and the body of the lambda you passed, instead of making a call.

17
00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:06.330
Here is that call site read back from the bytecode, as Java.

18
00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:09.856
There is no Function0, no invoke, and no unboxing.

19
00:01:09.856 --> 00:01:15.496
The multiply sits right inside the caller, between two calls to System.nanoTime.

20
00:01:15.667 --> 00:01:18.270
Read a bit further and you see the price.

21
00:01:18.270 --> 00:01:24.939
The finally block is in the bytecode twice: once for the normal exit, and once for the exit by exception.

22
00:01:25.167 --> 00:01:27.228
Every caller carries that copy.

23
00:01:27.228 --> 00:01:30.620
So inline is for small functions that take lambdas.

24
00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:33.080
The standard library is full of them.

25
00:01:33.867 --> 00:01:35.568
Java gets none of this.

26
00:01:35.568 --> 00:01:37.862
javac can't inline Kotlin code.

27
00:01:37.862 --> 00:01:46.815
To Java, timed is an ordinary static method that takes a Function0, and the Java lambda becomes an object like any other.

28
00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:48.552
One last rule.

29
00:01:48.552 --> 00:01:53.992
Pasting only works when the lambda runs right there, inside the inline function.

30
00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:58.403
Store the lambda in a list, and the compiler asks for noinline.

31
00:01:58.403 --> 00:02:00.805
That one lambda stays a real object.

32
00:02:01.033 --> 00:02:04.764
Call it from inside a Runnable, and it asks for crossinline.

33
00:02:04.764 --> 00:02:11.479
The body is still pasted, into a class generated for that call site, and a bare return is not allowed in it.
